Draft:
Recent threat activity has been characterized by the use of fileless malware, malicious DLLs, and socially engineered malware. This suggests that adversaries are increasingly utilizing flexible and adaptable tactics to evade detection and compromise systems. To mitigate these threats, several measures can be employed. Organizations should ensure they have arbitrary code protection capabilities in place to prevent malicious code from being injected into trusted processes. Integrating Windows Defender Application Guard can also help to isolate and protect high-risk applications and browsing sessions. Furthermore, enabling Windows Defender SmartScreen can provide additional protection against socially engineered malware by warning users of potentially malicious downloads. Additionally, utilizing PowerShell Constrained Language Mode can limit the actions that PowerShell can perform, reducing the attack surface. Finally, deploying Hypervisor Code Integrity (HVCI) can help to enforce the integrity of kernel-mode drivers, preventing malicious drivers from being loaded into the system. By implementing these measures, organizations can better defend against these evolving threats.

